SE: 43 seconds] Tutor's cursor points to the 30-degree angle.\nTutor draws a box around the 48° and n angles.\nTutor writes \"48 + n = 180\" on the whiteboard. Then, below the \"48\", the tutor writes \"-48\" and draws a line underneath, writing \"0\". To the right of the equation, the tutor writes \"= -48\" and then \"132\" below it.\nTutor clicks the \"Next\" button.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Or if you want some help, I could also chime in.\nTurn 35. STUDENT: Sure.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: Okay. So, you're given angle 48, angle n, and angle 30. In this problem, angle 30 we don't really have to mind because we're just looking at the size of angle n, which is a part of this, um, this section right here. And you know that this is a straight line and that all angles that are on a straight line when they're added together equal 180.\nTurn 37. TUTOR: So, when you add 48 and n, it has to equal 180. And to solve for n, we need to get rid of everything on this side of the equation that's not a letter. So then we have to subtract 48 by itself. Then we get zero here. And then we subtract 48 on this side from 180. And then we get 132, which is the answer.\nTurn 38. STUDENT: Oh.\nTurn 39. TUTOR: Do you see that?\nTurn 40. STUDENT: Yeah. Yeah.\nTurn 41. TUTOR: Okay. So, let's see this next one.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p2 - 'Who is correct?' (start)] A new problem appears. It reads: \"Tom and Katie are arguing about the diagram on the right. Tom says it shows p + q < 360°. Katie says it shows p + q = 270°. Who is correct?\". The diagram shows a point with three angles around it: p, q, and a right angle (indicated by a square symbol). The options are A: Only Tom, B: Only Katie, C: Both Tom and Katie, D: Neither is correct.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Okay, Tom and Katie are arguing about the diagram on the right. Tom says it shows p plus q is less than 360, but Katie says it shows p plus q is equal to 270. So, which person is right?\nTurn 43. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 52 seconds] Student clicks the \"Check\"